From: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 17:30:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: tag SOE hop descriptors with the SA index only

The SOE uses bits 7:5 of hop descriptor byte 28 when it computes the
ESP integrity check value. The driver stored a per-request completion
sequence in these bits, and the reserved value 7 for TCP and UDP
packets that a bound PPE entry might consume. Only a zero value gives a
valid ICV, so every packet submitted with a non-zero sequence and every
untracked TCP or UDP packet left the engine with a correctly encrypted
payload but an ICV that the peer rejects.

Write only the SA index to byte 28 and match completions without a
per-request tag. The requests of an SA are queued under sa->lock in the
order in which they are handed to the single SOE QDMA TX ring, which
feeds one PSE output queue per direction, and an SA is used in one
direction only. Relying on the engine to return the results of a queue
in that order, each completion is matched with the oldest queued
context of its SA. A completion that the Ethernet driver drops still
consumes the oldest context, so explicit drops keep the queue aligned.
The limit of seven requests in flight per SA is kept.

A context that has been queued for longer than one second is treated
as lost and released before the next request of its SA is queued, and
deferring a request now kicks the deferral worker, whose retry of the
oldest deferred request releases the lost contexts of that request's
SA. A completion that never arrives therefore can neither stall
transmission nor shift the matching beyond the next idle period of its
SA. A completion that finds no queued context cannot be told apart from
the late result of such a context, so it is dropped instead of
disabling the provider, and a tag that is not a valid SA index is not
matched at all.

An untracked request cannot be expressed anymore, so TCP and UDP
packets are submitted like every other packet. A result consumed by a
bound PPE entry never produces a CPU completion, and the PPE builds the
hop of such packets itself, so mark every new SA as PPE-disabled.
airoha_soe_ppe_flow_add() then refuses outbound and inbound SOE flows,
no SOE flow is bound in the PPE and the bind barrier is never armed.
Every submitted packet produces exactly one CPU completion.

Signed-off-by: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
